			<div class="content" id="post-content-1921140">
									

<p>I don't care for this one. I don't like the expungement at all (why do you need to redact the last numeral of the year this appeared? Or the edition of textbooks this appears in, since you've already blackboxed the title?), the fact that someone has to go looking for a specific blackboxed doctor to test this thing, or the latest iteration of &quot;lol the Foundation always uses SCP as an acronym get it?&quot;.</p>
<p>The item itself doesn't grab me conceptually, either. So something is harvesting brain tissue from people who pass the math problem intelligence test. Why does it bother sending back scary mangled corpses with cliched &quot;written on skin&quot; messages? Wouldn't that be completely counterproductive? It obviously wants more people to read the calculus problem, which is probably not going to happen if the textbook happens to be next to some crazy cubified corpse thing. The whole thing feels completely tacked-on for the purposes of horror.</p>
<p>The test logs also feel like filler, and don't really tell us anything that we didn't already get from the article other than that whatever it is that's processing these people doesn't like it when you send them the non-mathematically inclined.</p>

